### Patient Demographics
* Identifier: Patient S.K.
* Age: 34 years
* Gender: Male
* Weight: 72 kg
* Medical History: Focal epilepsy (newly diagnosed), no prior drug allergies, no chronic co-morbidities.

### Clinical Chronology
* Month 1 (Day 1): Diagnosed with focal epilepsy; initiated on Carbamazepine 200 mg orally twice daily.
* Month 1 (Day 18): Patient developed low-grade fever, malaise, and sore throat.
* Month 1 (Day 21): Developed painful erythematous macules on the face and trunk, conjunctival injection, and oral mucosal erosions.
* Month 1 (Day 22): Epidermal detachment covered 8 percent of total body surface area (TBSA). Presented to the emergency department.
* Laboratory Findings at Admission:
  * White Blood Cell Count: 11,200 cells/mcL with mild eosinophilia (6 percent)
  * Serum Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL (Normal reference range: 0.7 to 1.3 mg/dL)
  * Alanine Aminotransferase (ALT): 85 U/L (Elevated; reference range: 7 to 56 U/L)
  * Skin Biopsy: Full-thickness epidermal necrosis with subepidermal blistering, confirming Stevens-Johnson Syndrome.
* Intervention: Immediate permanent discontinuation of Carbamazepine; patient transferred to the intensive care unit (ICU) for barrier nursing, aggressive IV fluid management, cyclosporine therapy, and supportive ophthalmic care.
* Follow-up (Day 14 post-discontinuation): Complete re-epithelialization of cutaneous lesions, resolution of mucosal ulcers, and normalization of liver enzymes.

